      "q": "24.\nShashi Kant has a vegetable garden in the shape of a rhombus. The length of each side of garden is $35\\;{\\text{m}}$ And Its diagonal is $42\\;{\\text{m}}$ long. After growing the vegetables in it. He wants to divide it in seven equal parts And look after each part once a week. Find the area of the garden which he has to look after daily.",
      "a": "Let\n${\\text{ABCD}}$\nbe garden\n$\\therefore {\\text{DC}} = 35\\;{\\text{m}}$\n${\\text{DB}} = 42\\;{\\text{m}}$\nDraw\n$$\\;CE \\bot DB$$\nThe diagonals of a rhombus bisect each other at right angles.\n$\\therefore {\\text{DE}} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\quad {\\text{DB}} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\times 42\\quad $\nor\n$21\\;{\\text{m}}$\nNow\n$C{E^2} = C{D^2} - D{E^2}$\n$ = {35^2} - {21^2}$\n$ = 784$\n${\\text{CE}} = 28\\;{\\text{m}}$\nArea of\n$\\Delta {\\text{DBC}}, = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\times DB \\times CE$\n$ = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\times 42 \\times 28$\n$ = 588\\,{\\text{sq}}\\,{\\text{cm}}$\n$\\therefore $\nArea of the garden\n${\\text{ABCD}} = 2 \\times 588\\,{\\text{sq}}\\,{\\text{m}}$\n$ = 1176\\,{\\text{sq}}\\,{\\text{m}}$\nArea of the garden he has to look after, daily\n$ = \\dfrac{{1176}}{7}\\,{\\text{sq}}\\,{\\text{m}}$\n$ = 168\\,{\\text{sq}}\\,{\\text{m}}$"
