      "q": "22. If diameter of a sphere is decreased by $25\\% $ then what percent does its curved surface area decrease?",
      "a": "Diameter of original sphere\n$ = {\\text{D}} = 2{\\text{R}}$\n$ \\Rightarrow {\\text{R}} = \\dfrac{{\\text{D}}}{2}$\nCurved surface area of original sphere\n$ = 4\\pi {{\\text{R}}^2}$\n$ = 4\\pi {\\left( {\\dfrac{{\\text{D}}}{2}} \\right)^2} = \\pi {{\\text{D}}^2}$\nAccording to the question, Decreased diameter\n$ = 25\\% $\nof\n${\\text{D}} = \\dfrac{{25}}{{100}}{\\text{D}}$\n$ = \\dfrac{{\\text{D}}}{4}$\n$\\therefore $\nDiameter of new sphere\n$ = {\\text{D}} - \\dfrac{{\\text{D}}}{4} = \\dfrac{{3{\\text{D}}}}{4}$\n$\\therefore $\nRadius of new sphere\n$ = \\dfrac{{3{\\text{D}}}}{8}$\nNow, curved surface area of new sphere\n$ = 4\\pi {r^2} = 4\\pi {\\left( {\\dfrac{{3{\\text{D}}}}{8}} \\right)^2}$\n$ = \\dfrac{{9\\pi }}{{16}}{{\\text{D}}^2}$\nChange in curved surface area\n$ = \\pi {{\\text{D}}^2} - \\dfrac{{9\\pi }}{{16}}{{\\text{D}}^2}$\n$ = \\dfrac{7}{{16}}\\pi {{\\text{D}}^2}$\nPercent change in the curved surface area\n$ = \\dfrac{{{\\text{ Change in curved surface area }}}}{{{\\text{ Curved surface area of origianal sphere }}}}$\n$ \\times 100$\n$ = \\dfrac{7}{{\\dfrac{7}{{16}}\\pi {{\\text{D}}^2}}}\\pi {{\\text{D}}^2} \\times 100$\n$ = \\dfrac{7}{{16}} \\times 100 = 43.75\\% $"
