      "q": "7. Prove that the sum of the deviations of an individual's observations from the mean is zero.",
      "a": "Let\n${{\\text{x}}_1},{{\\text{x}}_2},{{\\text{x}}_3}, \\cdots ,{{\\text{x}}_{\\text{n}}}$\nbe n\u00a0 individual observations whose mean is\n$\\bar x$\n. The sum of the deviations of these n observations from\n$\\overline x $\nis given by:\n$\\sum\\limits_{{\\text{i}} = 1}^n {\\left( {{x_{\\text{i}}} - \\overline x } \\right)}\u00a0 = \\left( {{x_i} - \\overline x } \\right) + \\left( {{x_2} - \\overline x } \\right) +\u00a0 \\ldots . + \\left( {{{\\text{x}}_n} - \\overline x } \\right)$\nAlso, we have\n$\\overline x\u00a0 = \\dfrac{1}{n}\\left[ {\\sum\\limits_{i = 1}^n {{x_i}} } \\right]$\n$ \\Rightarrow {\\text{n}}\\overline x\u00a0 = \\sum\\limits_{{\\text{l}} = 1}^n {{x_i}} $\nNow,\n$\\sum\\limits_{{\\text{i}} = 1}^n {\\left( {{x_{\\text{i}}} - \\overline x } \\right)}\u00a0 = \\left( {{x_i} - \\overline x } \\right) + \\left( {{x_2} - \\overline x } \\right) +\u00a0 \\ldots . + \\left( {{{\\text{x}}_n} - \\overline x } \\right)$\n$ = \\left( {{x_1} + {x_2} +\u00a0 \\ldots . + {x_n}} \\right) - n\\overline x $\n$ = \\sum\\limits_{{\\text{i}} - 1}^n {{x_{\\text{i}}}}\u00a0 - n\\overline x $\n$ = \\;n\\overline x \\; - \\;n\\overline x $\n= 0\nSo,\n$\\sum\\limits_{i = 1}^n {\\left( {{x_i} - \\overline x } \\right)}\u00a0 = 0$"
