      "q": "4. The water for a factory is stored in a hemispherical tank whose internal diameter is $14 m$. The tank contains $50$ kilolitres of water. Water is pumped into the tank to fill its capacity. Calculate the volume of water pumped into the tank.",
      "a": "Given,\nInternal diameter $= 14{\\text{m}}$\nThus, radius $r = \\dfrac{{14}}{2}$\n$\\Rightarrow r = 7{\\text{cm}}$\nCapacity of the tank will be volume of the hemisphere\nVolume of the hemisphere\n$= \\dfrac{2}{3}\\pi {r^3}$\n$\\Rightarrow V = \\dfrac{2}{3}\\pi {\\left( 7 \\right)^3}$\n$\\Rightarrow V = \\dfrac{2}{3} \\times \\dfrac{{22}}{7}\\left( {343} \\right)$\n$\\Rightarrow V = 718.67{\\text{c}}{{\\text{m}}^3}$\nWe know that 50 kilolitres = $50 {m^3}$\nVolume of water pumped into the tank\n$= $\nCapacity of the tank $ -50$\n$= 718.67 - 50$\n$= 668.66{\\text{ }}{{\\text{m}}^{\\text{3}}}$"
